# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  theourgia_tile.la — the tiling layout: a binary split tree (Stage 10).
#
#  A tiling window manager never places a window by hand: the screen is cut in
#  two, each half is cut again, and every window is one leaf of that tree. This
#  module is the tree and every operation the window manager performs on it:
#  open a window beside the focused one, close one, find the neighbour in a
#  direction, swap two windows, move a split, turn a split, and compute where
#  every window goes. It knows nothing about terminals or pixels; ids are just
#  positive integers.
#
#  THE TREE. EMPTY (no windows) | LEAF(id) | SPLIT(dir)(ratio)(first)(second).
#  dir 0 = side by side (first on the left), 1 = stacked (first on top); ratio
#  is the first child's share in permille. Scott-encoded, three cases:
#      t(la _. if_empty)(la id. if_leaf)(la dir. la ratio. la a. la b. if_split)
#  The window manager does not need to look inside: every use goes through the
#  kit's operations, and show(t) prints the canonical text form, for example
#  E, L1, H500(L1,V500(L2,L3))  (H = side by side, V = stacked).
#
#  USE. TILE_KIT(add)(sub)(mul)(div)(lt)(int_eq)(concat)(int_to_str) returns
#  the record (destructure it positionally, in THIS order):
#      la sel. sel(empty)(insert)(remove)(layout)(neighbour)(swap)(resize)
#                 (toggle)(leaves)(next)(has)(count)(show)
#  The operations, as WM_DESIGN.md specifies them:
#    empty                         the empty tree
#    insert(t)(focus)(id)(dir)     split leaf focus into
#                                  SPLIT(dir)(500)(LEAF focus)(LEAF id);
#                                  on EMPTY, LEAF id
#    remove(t)(id)                 drop leaf id; its sibling takes the parent's
#                                  place; the last leaf leaves EMPTY
#    layout(t)(x)(y)(w)(h)(gap)    Scott list of la k. k(id)(x)(y)(w)(h), leaves
#                                  in order (see LAYOUT below for the formula)
#    neighbour(t)(id)(d)(x)(y)(w)(h)(gap)
#                                  the window next to id in direction d (0 left,
#                                  1 right, 2 up, 3 down) in that layout, or id
#    swap(t)(a)(b)                 exchange the ids of leaves a and b
#    resize(t)(id)(delta)          grow id's share of its parent split by delta
#                                  permille (negative shrinks), the split's
#                                  ratio clamped to [100, 900]
#    toggle(t)(id)                 flip the dir of id's parent split
#    leaves(t)                     Scott list of the ids, in order
#    next(t)(id)                   the leaf after id, wrapping around
#    has(t)(id)                    Church boolean
#    count(t)                      number of leaves
#    show(t)                       canonical text
#
#  WHAT THE CONTRACT LEAVES OPEN, DEFINED HERE (the gate checks each one):
#    - An id that is not in the tree never corrupts it: insert with an absent
#      focus (on a non-empty tree), remove/resize/toggle of an absent id, and
#      swap where either id is absent all return the tree UNCHANGED; next and
#      neighbour of an absent id return that id. On EMPTY, insert ignores focus.
#    - insert keeps ids unique: an id already in the tree, or an id that is not
#      positive, leaves the tree unchanged.
#    - insert's dir: 0 is side by side, ANY other value is stacked (stored as 1),
#      so show is always H or V.
#    - resize/toggle of a leaf with no parent split (a lone LEAF, or EMPTY) is
#      unchanged. A second child's share is 1000 - ratio, so growing it by delta
#      lowers ratio by delta; the clamp [100, 900] is symmetric, so either share
#      stays in [100, 900].
#    - swap(t)(a)(a) is unchanged; next of the only leaf is that leaf.
#    - layout clamps negative w, h, gap to 0 first. At a side-by-side split of
#      width w the gap actually used is g = min(gap, w), so w1 = (w-g)*ratio/1000
#      (truncating), w2 = w-g-w1, second at x+w1+g: with gap <= w this is
#      exactly the contract's formula; with gap > w both children get width 0
#      and the second sits at the right edge, so no size goes negative and every
#      rectangle stays inside the area. Stacked splits do the same on h. So the
#      leaves and the gap strips always tile the area exactly.
#    - neighbour: R = id's rectangle. A candidate C (another leaf) must lie
#      wholly past R's edge in direction d (left: C.x+C.w <= R.x; right:
#      C.x >= R.x+R.w; up: C.y+C.h <= R.y; down: C.y >= R.y+R.h) and overlap R
#      by MORE than zero on the other axis. Nearest (the gap between the two
#      facing edges) wins, then the larger overlap, then the earlier leaf. A d
#      outside 0..3 has no candidates, so it returns id.
#
#  PERFORMANCE (WM_DESIGN.md "How fast the VM is"). Kit style: the builtins are
#  parameters, the constants are bound once when the kit is built, every
#  function is let-bound (LET and Z are named only while the kit is being
#  built), so after TILE_KIT returns, no operation names a glyph or a builtin.
#  Booleans are applied straight to values or to thunks and a dummy. Every
#  operation is one to three walks of the tree (insert = has + rebuild,
#  neighbour = layout + find + scan), and the trees are small (a screen holds
#  tens of windows, not thousands). Every operation but show does a constant
#  amount of work per node. show does one walk too, but concat copies both its
#  operands (WM_DESIGN.md rule 5), so each level copies the text built below
#  it again: a byte that lies d splits deep is copied 2 or 3 times a level, at
#  most 3d + 4 times in all (the gate checks this bound). That is O(n log n)
#  bytes on a balanced tree and O(n²) on a chain, the shape that splitting
#  the newest window again and again builds. It is NOT a balanced join, on
#  purpose: on this VM a list of pieces costs about a microsecond of closure
#  work per piece, more than the copying it saves on any tree a screen holds.
#  Measured (CPU time a call; a pairwise join of the text's pieces in
#  brackets): a 30-window chain 0.10 ms (0.22), 160 windows 0.85 ms (1.29),
#  320 windows 2.4 ms (2.3); a 320-window balanced tree 1.2 to 1.7 ms (2.9).
#  The window manager does not call show; it is the canonical form for tests.
#  Measured on the VM (CPU time a call, 10 and 30 windows, chain and balanced
#  alike): layout 0.09 and 0.30 ms, neighbour 0.21 and 0.65 ms, insert+remove
#  0.09 and 0.29 ms, swap, resize, toggle 0.05 and 0.10 to 0.16 ms, show 0.03
#  and 0.10 ms, next, has, leaves, count under 0.03 and 0.10 ms. A gdb trap on
#  the VM's glyph-table lookup counts the same names for 1 and 5 rounds of
#  every operation (and for 0 and 40 rounds of show): only the kit's own
#  construction reaches the table.
#
#  Pure Lingua Adamica: byte-identical on the C host and the native VM. The
#  host is much slower here (tens of ms an operation: its substitution copies
#  the kit's closures at every step), which is fine for tests, not for a WM.
#  gate_wm_tile.sh checks it against an independent Python model of the same
#  tree over a few hundred seeded random operations, hand-derived known
#  answers, and the layout invariants (disjoint, inside the area, an exact
#  tiling with the gaps, unique ids, count = length of leaves).
#
#  OUT OF SCOPE (honestly): no minimum window size (a split simply truncates;
#  at a tiny area windows get width or height 0), no floating windows, no
#  tabbed or stacked containers, and resize moves only the IMMEDIATE parent
#  split (i3 would look further up for a split in the matching direction).
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

export TILE_KIT

glyph Z   = la f. (la x. f(la v. x(x)(v)))(la x. f(la v. x(x)(v)))
glyph LET = la v. la f. f(v)

glyph TILE_KIT = la add. la sub. la mul. la div. la lt. la int_eq. la concat. la int_to_str.
  LET(0)(la C0. LET(1)(la C1. LET(2)(la C2. LET(3)(la C3.
  LET(100)(la C100. LET(500)(la C500. LET(900)(la C900. LET(1000)(la C1000.

  # ── encodings: Church booleans, Scott lists, the three tree cases ──
  LET(la t. la f. t)(la TT.
  LET(la t. la f. f)(la FF.
  LET(la n. la c. n(n))(la NIL.
  LET(la h. la tl. la n. la c. c(h)(tl))(la CONS.
  LET(la e. la l. la s. e(e))(la EMPTY.
  LET(la id. la e. la l. la s. l(id))(la LEAF.
  LET(la d. la r. la a. la b. la e. la l. la s. s(d)(r)(a)(b))(la SPLIT.
  LET(la a. la b. lt(a)(b)(a)(b))(la MIN.
  LET(la a. la b. lt(a)(b)(b)(a))(la MAX.
  LET(la v. lt(v)(C100)(C100)(lt(C900)(v)(C900)(v)))(la CLAMP.
  LET(la dir. int_eq(dir)(C0)(C0)(C1))(la NORMD.
  LET(la n. la id. n(la _. FF)(la i. int_eq(i)(id))(la d. la r. la a. la b. FF))(la ISLEAF.

  # ── queries ──
  LET(Z(la has. la t. la id.
        t(la _. FF)
         (la i. int_eq(i)(id))
         (la d. la r. la a. la b. has(a)(id)(la _. TT)(la _. has(b)(id))(C0))))(la HAS.
  LET(Z(la count. la t.
        t(la _. C0)(la i. C1)(la d. la r. la a. la b. add(count(a))(count(b)))))(la COUNT.
  LET(Z(la go. la t. la acc.
        t(la _. acc)(la i. CONS(i)(acc))(la d. la r. la a. la b. go(a)(go(b)(acc)))))(la LVS.
  LET(la t. LVS(t)(NIL))(la LEAVES.
  # SHOW: ((head ++ (show(a) ++ ",")) ++ (show(b) ++ ")")). concat copies both
  # operands, so the order matters: this copies the second child's text twice
  # and the first child's three times per level (nesting the other way round,
  # concat(head)(concat(show(a))(concat(",")(...))), copies them 6 and 4
  # times). See PERFORMANCE in the header for why it is not a balanced join.
  LET(Z(la show. la t.
        t(la _. "E")
         (la i. concat("L")(int_to_str(i)))
         (la d. la r. la a. la b.
            concat(concat(concat(int_eq(d)(C0)("H")("V"))(concat(int_to_str(r))("(")))
                         (concat(show(a))(",")))
                  (concat(show(b))(")")))))(la SHOW.
  # NX(first)(id)(l): the element after id in l, first if id is last, id if absent.
  LET(Z(la nx. la first. la id. la l.
        l(la _. id)
         (la h. la tl. int_eq(h)(id)
             (la _. tl(la _. first)(la h2. la t2. h2))
             (la _. nx(first)(id)(tl))(C0))))(la NX.
  LET(la t. la id. (la l. l(la _. id)(la h. la tl. NX(h)(id)(l)))(LEAVES(t)))(la NEXT.

  # ── edits ──
  # MAPL(f)(t): t with every leaf n (id i) replaced by f(i)(n).
  LET(Z(la m. la f. la t.
        t(la _. t)(la i. f(i)(t))(la d. la r. la a. la b. SPLIT(d)(r)(m(f)(a))(m(f)(b)))))(la MAPL.
  LET(la t. la focus. la id. la dir.
        lt(C0)(id)
          (la _. HAS(t)(id)
             (la _. t)
             (la _. (la ins. t(la _. LEAF(id))(la i. ins(C0))(la d. la r. la a. la b. ins(C0)))
                      (la _. MAPL(la i. la n. int_eq(i)(focus)(la _. SPLIT(NORMD(dir))(C500)(n)(LEAF(id)))
                                                            (la _. n)(C0))(t)))
             (C0))
          (la _. t)(C0))(la INSERT.
  # RM(id)(t): a leaf child equal to id is replaced by its sibling; the root
  # leaf id becomes EMPTY. Ids are unique, so at most one leaf matches.
  LET(Z(la rm. la id. la t.
        t(la _. t)
         (la i. int_eq(i)(id)(EMPTY)(t))
         (la d. la r. la a. la b.
            ISLEAF(a)(id)(la _. b)
              (la _. ISLEAF(b)(id)(la _. a)(la _. SPLIT(d)(r)(rm(id)(a))(rm(id)(b)))(C0))(C0))))(la RM.
  LET(la t. la id. RM(id)(t))(la REMOVE.
  LET(la t. la a. la b.
        HAS(t)(a)
          (la _. HAS(t)(b)
             (la _. MAPL(la i. la n. int_eq(i)(a)(LEAF(b))(int_eq(i)(b)(LEAF(a))(n)))(t))
             (la _. t)(C0))
          (la _. t)(C0))(la SWAP.
  # ATP(fa)(fb)(id)(t): the split whose FIRST child is leaf id becomes
  # fa(d)(r)(a)(b), the one whose SECOND child is leaf id becomes fb(d)(r)(a)(b).
  LET(Z(la ap. la fa. la fb. la id. la t.
        t(la _. t)(la i. t)
         (la d. la r. la a. la b.
            ISLEAF(a)(id)(la _. fa(d)(r)(a)(b))
              (la _. ISLEAF(b)(id)(la _. fb(d)(r)(a)(b))
                 (la _. SPLIT(d)(r)(ap(fa)(fb)(id)(a))(ap(fa)(fb)(id)(b)))(C0))(C0))))(la ATP.
  LET(la t. la id. la delta.
        ATP(la d. la r. la a. la b. SPLIT(d)(CLAMP(add(r)(delta)))(a)(b))
           (la d. la r. la a. la b. SPLIT(d)(CLAMP(sub(r)(delta)))(a)(b))(id)(t))(la RESIZE.
  LET(la t. la id.
        (la flip. ATP(flip)(flip)(id)(t))
          (la d. la r. la a. la b. SPLIT(int_eq(d)(C0)(C1)(C0))(r)(a)(b)))(la TOGGLE.

  # ── layout ──
  # LAY(gap)(t)(x)(y)(w)(h)(acc): t's rectangles consed in front of acc.
  LET(Z(la go. la gap. la t. la x. la y. la w. la h. la acc.
        t(la _. acc)
         (la i. CONS(la k. k(i)(x)(y)(w)(h))(acc))
         (la d. la r. la a. la b.
            int_eq(d)(C0)
              (la _. (la g. (la av. (la w1.
                        go(gap)(a)(x)(y)(w1)(h)(go(gap)(b)(add(x)(add(w1)(g)))(y)(sub(av)(w1))(h)(acc)))
                      (div(mul(av)(r))(C1000)))(sub(w)(g)))(MIN(gap)(w)))
              (la _. (la g. (la av. (la h1.
                        go(gap)(a)(x)(y)(w)(h1)(go(gap)(b)(x)(add(y)(add(h1)(g)))(w)(sub(av)(h1))(acc)))
                      (div(mul(av)(r))(C1000)))(sub(h)(g)))(MIN(gap)(h)))(C0))))(la LAY.
  LET(la t. la x. la y. la w. la h. la gap.
        LAY(MAX(C0)(gap))(t)(x)(y)(MAX(C0)(w))(MAX(C0)(h))(NIL))(la LAYOUT.

  # ── neighbour ──
  # FINDR(id)(l): la n. la s. … — s(rect) for id's rectangle in l, else n(n).
  LET(Z(la fd. la id. la l.
        l(la _. la n. la s. n(n))
         (la c. la tl. c(la i. la x. la y. la w. la h.
            int_eq(i)(id)(la _. la n. la s. s(c))(la _. fd(id)(tl))(C0)))))(la FINDR.
  # NBDIR(d)(rx)(ry)(rw)(rh): la n. la s. … — s(dist)(overlap) for direction d,
  # n(n) if d is not 0..3. dist(c) < 0 means c is not wholly past the edge.
  LET(la dir. la rx. la ry. la rw. la rh.
        (la ovx. la ovy.
          int_eq(dir)(C0)
            (la _. la n. la s. s(la c. c(la i. la x. la y. la w. la h. sub(rx)(add(x)(w))))(ovy))
            (la _. int_eq(dir)(C1)
              (la _. la n. la s. s(la c. c(la i. la x. la y. la w. la h. sub(x)(add(rx)(rw))))(ovy))
              (la _. int_eq(dir)(C2)
                (la _. la n. la s. s(la c. c(la i. la x. la y. la w. la h. sub(ry)(add(y)(h))))(ovx))
                (la _. int_eq(dir)(C3)
                  (la _. la n. la s. s(la c. c(la i. la x. la y. la w. la h. sub(y)(add(ry)(rh))))(ovx))
                  (la _. la n. la s. n(n))(C0))(C0))(C0))(C0))
        (la c. c(la i. la x. la y. la w. la h. sub(MIN(add(rx)(rw))(add(x)(w)))(MAX(rx)(x))))
        (la c. c(la i. la x. la y. la w. la h. sub(MIN(add(ry)(rh))(add(y)(h)))(MAX(ry)(y)))))(la NBDIR.
  # BETTER(found)(dd)(ov)(bd)(bo): does (dd, ov) beat the best so far (bd, bo)?
  LET(la found. la dd. la ov. la bd. la bo.
        found(la _. lt(dd)(bd)(la _. TT)(la _. int_eq(dd)(bd)(la _. lt(bo)(ov))(la _. FF)(C0))(C0))
             (la _. TT)(C0))(la BETTER.
  # SCAN(id)(dist)(ovl)(l)(found)(bi)(bd)(bo): the best candidate's id, or bi.
  LET(Z(la sc. la id. la dist. la ovl. la l. la found. la bi. la bd. la bo.
        l(la _. bi)
         (la c. la tl.
            c(la ci. la cx. la cy. la cw. la ch.
              (la skip.
                int_eq(ci)(id)
                  (la _. skip(C0))
                  (la _. (la dd. lt(dd)(C0)
                      (la _. skip(C0))
                      (la _. (la ov. lt(C0)(ov)
                          (la _. BETTER(found)(dd)(ov)(bd)(bo)
                                   (la _. sc(id)(dist)(ovl)(tl)(TT)(ci)(dd)(ov))
                                   (la _. skip(C0))(C0))
                          (la _. skip(C0))(C0))(ovl(c)))(C0))(dist(c)))(C0))
              (la _. sc(id)(dist)(ovl)(tl)(found)(bi)(bd)(bo))))))(la SCAN.
  LET(la t. la id. la dir. la x. la y. la w. la h. la gap.
        (la l. FINDR(id)(l)
            (la _. id)
            (la rr. rr(la ri. la rx. la ry. la rw. la rh.
                NBDIR(dir)(rx)(ry)(rw)(rh)
                  (la _. id)
                  (la dist. la ovl. SCAN(id)(dist)(ovl)(l)(FF)(id)(C0)(C0)))))
          (LAYOUT(t)(x)(y)(w)(h)(gap)))(la NEIGHBOUR.

  la sel. sel(EMPTY)(INSERT)(REMOVE)(LAYOUT)(NEIGHBOUR)(SWAP)(RESIZE)(TOGGLE)
             (LEAVES)(NEXT)(HAS)(COUNT)(SHOW)
  ))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
